  "client_view_text": "  Feel free to use examples or analogies.\n\nConsider a database that's using a write-ahead log.  At the time of a crash, the database has committed most of the files to disk, but there's a few transactions that aren't yet fully persisted.  The database has also written the transactions to the log on disk before committing the underlying files.  In this scenario, the database can restore to a consistent state by replaying the log after a crash.\n\nThink of it like a river with multiple tributaries.  Each tributary represents updates to the database.  The river itself is the write-ahead log, which A write-ahead log is a file in which database modifications are recorded before they are written to the database files or disk storage. Here are the key points about how a write-ahead log allows for recovery after a crash:\nA write-ahead log is a permanent, sequential file that stores all modifications to the database information before they are committed to the database files. This record of modifications can be used by the database to recover all changes that were made before the crash.\nAfter a crash, the database uses the write-ahead log to determine which changes should be recovered. The write-ahead log contains a sequence of modifications (insertions, deletions, updates) that were made to the database before the crash. The database applies these modifications to the database files.\nThis process is called rolling forward. The database is essentially replaying all the changes that were recorded in the write-ahead log, making sure they are applied correctly to the database files. This ensures that any transactions that were in progress when the crash occurred are either completed successfully or rolled back, if they were incomplete at the time of the crash.\nThe write-ahead log also helps the database to roll back any transactions that were in progress at the time of the crash. The log contains information about the start and end of transactions, as well as any"
